# Copy bank

Use this file to replace hedged or AI-sounding text.

## Hero

Title:

```text
China Chip Tooling Talent Monitor
```

Subtitle:

```text
What public records reveal about the people and organizations behind China’s semiconductor-equipment push.
```

Body:

```text
The monitor tracks firms, city clusters, role families, and source records tied to etch/clean, deposition, metrology/inspection, and lithography-adjacent tooling. It is built for analysts watching how Chinese toolmakers convert R&D into production support.
```

Small beta note:

```text
Beta dataset: 43 sources, 172 evidence rows. Counts track public records, not workforce size.
```

## Section titles

Replace:

```text
What the visible layer does, and does not, show.
```

With:

```text
Four reads from the public record
```

Replace:

```text
A schematic read of where evidence shows up.
```

With:

```text
Where public records concentrate
```

Replace:

```text
An evidence ladder, not a leaderboard.
```

With:

```text
Three tiers of evidence
```

Replace:

```text
How different readers should use the atlas.
```

With:

```text
Who this helps
```

Replace:

```text
What three listed equipment firms actually disclose.
```

With:

```text
What AMEC, ACM Research, and NAURA publish about their workforce
```

## Better caveat style

Bad headline:

```text
An evidence ladder, not a leaderboard.
```

Better heading:

```text
Three tiers of evidence
```

Small note under the exhibit:

```text
Rows are grouped by source strength. They are not rolled into a score.
```

Bad:

```text
Treat the city view as a navigation layer for evidence, not as a geographic scorecard.
```

Better:

```text
The city layer points analysts to records worth checking first.
```

Bad:

```text
The brief should make absence legible instead of filling it with invented precision.
```

Better:

```text
The missing production layer is the main reason product claims remain hard to read.
```

## Analyst voice section

Add this to methodology or the essay:

```text
I read Chinese public sources as records of visibility, not ground truth. A local shortage list tells me what a city wants to attract or subsidize. A product page tells me how a firm wants its capabilities understood. A filing is stronger, but even filings compress roles that matter in practice: applications engineering, field service, customer ramp, and production recovery. This monitor is built around that tension.
```

## One-sentence product pitch

```text
A public-record monitor of the firms, cities, role families, and source signals behind China’s semiconductor-equipment talent push.
```

## Boss demo opening

```text
I built this as a prototype for monitoring China’s semiconductor-equipment ecosystem through the talent layer. The point is not to claim we can measure capability directly. The point is to track the public records that show whether Chinese toolmakers are building the R&D, service, application, and training structures that help turn product claims into production support.
```
</file>

<file path="docs/v4/china-tooling-atlas-v4-implementation-pack/05_demo_script.md">
# Demo script for DGA / employer conversations

## 30-second version

```text
I built a beta evidence product on China’s semiconductor-equipment talent system. Most analysis tracks export controls, product announcements, and market share. I wanted to track the people and organizations behind tool catch-up: R&D teams, service capacity, role families, city clusters, shortage lists, and firm disclosures. The result is a public-record monitor with a source ledger, firm dossiers, segment briefs, and an explorer.
```

## 2-minute walkthrough

1. Start on the homepage.

```text
The product starts from one question: what should an analyst watch to know whether Chinese toolmakers are converting R&D into production support?
```

2. Point to the findings.

```text
The first finding is a source discipline point: 38 percent of rows are structural scaffolding, not field evidence. I keep that visible because broad STEM and discipline counts are easy to overread.
```

3. Open AMEC or NAURA.

```text
This is the next-click layer. If a user sees AMEC, they can open a firm dossier rather than falling into a raw table. The dossier explains what the firm does, which tool segments it touches, what workforce categories it discloses, what to watch, and what not to infer.
```

4. Open a segment page.

```text
The segment pages translate technical bottlenecks into role families. Etch is not deposition; metrology is not lithography. Each segment needs different combinations of plasma, chemistry, optics, algorithms, calibration, service, and applications engineering.
```

5. Open the map.

```text
The map is not a talent ranking. It shows where public records concentrate and where an analyst might start checking city-level sources.
```

6. Open the explorer and methodology.

```text
The explorer keeps the work auditable. Every row traces back to a source. The methodology keeps the claims bounded: public visibility, not capability.
```

## What this shows about you

```text
This project shows I can scope a messy strategic-technology problem, build a defensible evidence model, create an interactive product, and translate technical details into client-facing analysis.
```

## For DGA specifically

```text
This could become a reusable framework for monitoring China critical-tech bottlenecks beyond chip tooling: advanced packaging, data-center power and thermal systems, critical minerals processing, and other areas where public records reveal partial but useful signals.
```
</file>

<file path="docs/v4/china-tooling-atlas-v4-implementation-pack/06_final_qa_checklist.md">
# Final QA checklist

## Build checks

Run:

```bash
npm run build:data
npm run lint
npm run build
```

## Homepage checks

- The first screen says what the product is.
- The word "beta" is present but not dominant.
- The first scroll contains a finding, not a disclaimer.
- There is a clear next click for AMEC, NAURA, ACM Research Shanghai.
- The city map has a visible mainland China silhouette.
- No homepage section title uses "not a X".

## Click-through checks

- AMEC card opens `/firms/amec`.
- NAURA card opens `/firms/naura`.
- ACM card opens `/firms/acm-research-shanghai`.
- Segment card opens `/segments/deposition` or equivalent.
- Map click opens `/explorer?city=Shanghai`.
- Explorer pre-fills URL query params.
- Source links open in a new tab.

## Language checks

Search the codebase for:

```bash
grep -R "why this matters\|evidence pattern\|actually\|sharpens\|surfaces\|leverage\|robust\|not a" app components data/editorial
```

Do not automatically delete every result. Inspect each one. Some words may be fine in methodology, but they should not dominate the homepage.

## Data claim checks

- No new number was added without a source ID.
- Firm pages do not claim segment-specific headcount.
- Map does not claim talent ranking.
- Explorer still shows audit details.
- Methodology still explains limits.

## Deployment checks

After pushing to GitHub:

- Vercel build passes.
- Open deployed homepage.
- Open one firm page.
- Open one segment page.
- Open `/explorer?city=Shanghai`.
- Open `/sources`.
- Test mobile width in browser dev tools.

## Final release label

Use:

```text
Beta evidence product
```

Avoid:

```text
real-time monitor
capability index
talent ranking
complete atlas
```
</file>

